Multikernel VSOCK receive context
================================

MK_MSG_NETWORK callbacks run under the raw IPI drain lock. Allocating an skb
with GFP_ATOMIC or taking an ordinary skb-queue spinlock there can violate
the lock nesting rules. The callback now validates the message/header lengths
and copies the complete packet into a preallocated record FIFO under a raw
lock. It does not retain the shared IPI slot, allocate/free an skb, or enter
the socket stack. The payload format and stream operations are unchanged.

The FIFO occupies MK_IPI_RING_SIZE * MK_MAX_DATA_SIZE bytes (256 KiB), with a
two-byte prefix per record. It holds 64 maximum-size packets or more smaller
packets. Full capacity drops a whole new packet without overwriting older
records or claiming success. A send accepted by the IPI transport still does
not guarantee remote socket delivery; overflow or allocation failure can cause
a connection to time out. This change does not add stream retransmission or
repair the transport's simplified flow control.

The existing work item is the sole consumer. It peeks the head length under
the raw lock, allocates in process context, then copies/removes that same head.
Producers only append; they cannot change it during allocation. Allocation
failure discards that record and continues. Each invocation consumes at most
64 records and requeues if needed. The fixed callback and workqueue preserve
workqueue non-reentrancy, including a receive racing the worker's final empty
check. No packet-sized kernel stack buffer is introduced.

Initialization creates the queue and registers the transport before publishing
the message callback; failure unwinds those steps. Exit unregisters/drains the
callback first, cancels work and destroys the queue, then unregisters the
transport. No worker can enter the socket stack after transport removal.
Queued raw bytes own no skb or external reference and need no allocator cleanup.

Run:

  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/vsock_rx_test.sh
  CC=clang CFLAGS='-O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all' \
    bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/vsock_rx_test.sh

The fixture extracts the actual receive/worker/init/exit functions and wire
header. It checks malformed input, copied data lifetime, FIFO saturation and
reuse, worker budget, allocation failure, an arrival during allocation and at
the final empty check, initialization errors, and entered callbacks during
unregister. FIFO, workqueue, IRQ and socket boundaries are modeled. Native
qualification must enable CONFIG_VSOCKETS and CONFIG_MULTIKERNEL_VSOCKETS;
passing lifecycle tests with those options disabled is not VSOCK evidence.
This fixture does not qualify socket protocol races, full streams, physical
hardware, peer identity/reuse, or real module unload concurrency.
